  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAnother murky forecast period lies ahead with respect to \nthunderstorms today. First, the heat advisory was expanded once \nagain to encompass remaining northern Indiana counties and a \nmajority of northwest Ohio counties (exempt are Williams and \nFulton). Michigan is also exempt. Heat indicies once again approach \n100F this afternoon. Observations on Thursday showed some sites \nreaching heat advisory criteria by noon, if not the 11am hour. High \nclouds this morning could briefly temper heat indicies, but \nafternoon sun could easily flip the script. While duration of \nadvisory criteria may be limited today in some areas, we can then \nplay the consecutive-days card as we stare down the prospect of our \n4th consecutive very hot day. \n\nOngoing this morning is an increasingly mature area of thunderstorms \nover central WI. Forecast guidance generally suggests this cluster \nwill weaken as it crosses into western Michigan after sunrise.\nHowever, the instability gradient could lead any lingering storms \ninto southern Michigan while effect shear of 40 knots could aid in \nthunderstorm maintenance. This is at odds with lacking lapse rates \nless than 7 C/km. Confidence is low on how these storms evolve for \nthe remainder of the morning, but they will be monitored closely \nprior to sunrise. \n\nLike 24 hours ago, our marginal risk of severe thunderstorms was \nupgraded to a slight risk this morning. This is primarily driven by \nthe anticipated development of thunderstorms this evening amid a \nstrengthening 500-mb jet in which we lie within the right-entracne \nregion. The low-level jet strengthens as well. Timing is nearly a \ntoss up in this challenging, nuanced mesoscale environment, yet \nsometime after 8pm EDT appears probable for storms to redevelop. \nConfidence is low, but damaging wind gusts and hail would be the \nprimary hazards from any severe storms today and tonight. \n\nDrying out Saturday in the wake of any early-morning lingering \nthunderstorms and not quite as hot as the upper-level jet buckles. \nBy Sunday, Canadian high pressure seeps in from the northeast, \nshunting the upper-level ridge out of our area for a time and \nbringing us a period of dry weather as well. Look for surface low \npressure to cross the Canadian Prairie midweek, with a cold front \ntracking across the northern and Central Plains. This cold front \nlooks to bring rather uniform coverage of showers and thunderstorms \nto our area around midweek. Beyond Wednesday, there is varying \nmagnitudes of our well-discussed upper-level ridge, now displaced \nthrough the mid-Mississippi Valley. This ridge may build back north \nbringing continued warmth, or, the cold front could hang-up atop \nthis ridge keeping us in line for additional late-week storms.",
